.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd September 6, 2026
.Dt SYS-INIT-CHECK-DISTRIBUTION 1
.Os sys-init experimental
.Sh NAME
.Nm sys-init-check-distribution
.Nd verify pinned offline Aether distribution reference inputs
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm python3 docs/check-distribution.py
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This is a developer command in the sys-init source checkout, not an installed
operator executable.
Run with Python 3.10 or newer from the repository root.
Inputs are resolved relative to the wrapper's location.
It reads the local
distribution.lock, verifies selected vendored release bytes and then executes
that
release's conformance/check.py with the invoking Python executable.
No sibling checkout or network source is used and no release is installed.
.Pp
The wrapper requires the exact lock field set, fixed format and source
project,
a vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH version, a matching distribution/version release path and
a lowercase 64-digit manifest digest.
Duplicate fields are rejected.
It rejects symlinks in the selected release root or inventory, validates the
manifest grammar and safe unique paths, hashes every listed file, and requires
the complete file inventory plus conformance/check.py before executing it.
The pinned checker runs offline descriptor, graph, trust-composition and
measurement reference cases, not native or cryptographic enforcement.
.Pp
The current lock selects v0.1.0.
A pass verifies only the stated offline scope;
it does not certify sys-init runtime conformance, approve publication or adopt
an unpublished standards candidate.
Restore reviewed bytes when integrity fails;
do not edit immutable release files or replace a digest merely to hide
failure.
.Sh ARGUMENTS
There is no command-line parser, help mode, version mode, output option or
release-selection operand.
All arguments are ignored, including --help,
--version and unknown arguments; they still run verification and the selected
checker.
Use the no-argument form.
Change a pin only through a separate adoption
review, not by passing an argument or pointing at a mutable sibling tree.
.Sh OUTPUT
The pinned reference checker output is captured as separate raw streams
and replayed after capture ends.
Output delivery is buffered.
The wrapper does not add a JSON envelope or
create a report directory.
Diagnostics use standard error; exceptions from
malformed input, missing files or a failing child can produce a traceback.
Caught ValueError, OSError and KeyError become short exit messages.
A failing subprocess raises CalledProcessError outside that catch and may
produce a traceback instead of preserving the child's numeric exit status.
.Pp
The reference child has a 30-second deadline and a combined 4 MiB output cap.
Use python3 scripts/check-all.py for the existing 60-second gate watchdog and
retained stdout/stderr.
A missing or interrupted result is not success.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
The child uses the same Python executable with -I -S -B.
Python environment options and import paths, user site and automatic site
initialization are excluded; assertions are enabled and bytecode disabled.
Non-Python environment values and the working directory are inherited.
The parent interpreter is unchanged and remains a trusted host dependency.
Temporary storage uses Python tempfile selection and is cleaned on unwinding.
There is no downloader or OS sandbox.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa docs/distribution.lock
Local key=value consumer pin.
This is an adoption input, not a service file.
.It Pa docs/vendor/aether-distribution/v0.1.0/SHA256SUMS
Currently selected manifest.
Its exact raw bytes are hashed by the lock.
.It Pa docs/vendor/aether-distribution/v0.1.0/
Offline release files.
The lock version selects this directory.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
The wrapper and invoked reference checker completed normally.
Arguments are not validated, so this is not a help or usage acknowledgement.
.It 1
An explicit integrity rejection or ordinary uncaught Python/subprocess
failure.
Other interpreter or external-termination statuses remain possible.
.It 130
Python interruption, including SIGTERM handled during main-thread capture.
.El
.Pp
There is no separate usage-error status or stable wrapper failure JSON.
.Sh EXAMPLES
From the repository root, with the tracked offline inputs present and ordinary
assert-enabled Python execution:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
python3 docs/check-distribution.py
.Ed
.Pp
Expect the selected release's text results and exit zero if its checks pass.
No root privileges are required.
For logs retained by the aggregate runner:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
python3 scripts/check-all.py
.Ed
.Pp
Do not use --help as a harmless query; it executes the same release checker.
Do not alter release bytes to demonstrate a negative example in this checkout.
.Sh SEE ALSO
Repository references: docs/DISTRIBUTION.md, docs/STANDARDS-RC1-REVIEW.md,
docs/DEVELOPMENT-CHECKS.md and docs/DOCUMENTATION-INVENTORY.md.
The selected offline release README explains its reference scope.
.Sh CAVEATS
Independent technical/reader review and target manual lookup remain pending.
Unpublished candidates remain review inputs.
No pin upgrade follows from this
manual, and no shared renderer or runtime conformance is claimed.
.Pp
Decision 0120 limits each lock, manifest and payload to 1 MiB and release
traversal to 2,000 entries including directories.
Locks use ASCII text; ordinary files without symlink components are required.
Nonblocking reads check descriptor identity and post-read size
and modification time.
These bounds do not provide an I/O deadline
or atomic verification and execution.
See docs/OFFLINE-LOCKS.md for the input contract and remaining limits.
.Pp
Decision 0121 rechecks lock/manifest bytes, complete release membership
and payload digests before invocation and after a successful child.
Persistent changes cause failure; child output may already have been printed.
Use the wrapper exit status to determine success.
Changes restored between comparisons and atomic execution remain outside scope.
.Pp
Decision 0122 runs verified payloads from a private temporary release tree,
with a combined 64 MiB payload limit.
The child uses Python -I -S -B, ignoring Python environment options and paths,
skipping site initialization and suppressing bytecode writes.
Assertions remain enabled for all families.
Temporary copies are checked and cleaned on normal or error unwinding.
This is not an OS sandbox or a guarantee of cleanup after abrupt termination.
.Pp
Decision 0123 bounds pipe draining and child exit together.
Timeout and excess output fail; accepted raw prefixes are still replayed.
Capture failure kills the owned process group and reaps the direct child
before temporary-file cleanup.
The deadline does not bound staging, filesystem I/O or blocked output replay.
Escaped descendants and abrupt outer termination remain outside this guarantee.
.Pp
Decision 0124 maps active main-thread capture SIGTERM to interruption,
with owned-group cleanup and direct-child reaping.
The CLI prints reference check interrupted on stderr and exits 130.
The previous handler is restored before output replay and staged-tree unwinding;
background-thread calls do not change handlers.
Startup, staging, postchecks and repeated signals after restoration remain
outside this termination scope.
